      "value": "> **Status:** 🟡 In Development\n> **Owner:** Integration Lead\n> **Last updated:** June 2026\n> **Registry entry:** [05.5 — Agent Registry](../278921217)\n\n---\n\n1. Purpose & Scope\n------------------\n\nThis TSD describes the architecture, component boundaries, and governance constraints for the AI-Assisted Defect Triage Agent — a hybrid agent that automates defect classification and fix-branch creation following failed BrowserStack Automate runs on the Zimmermann SFCC replatform programme.\n\n**In scope:**\n- CI-automated triage pipeline (GitHub Actions + AWS Bedrock)\n- Tier classification logic and routing decisions\n- Automated Jira defect creation and GitHub fix-branch creation\n- Claude Code CLI skill for interactive investigation and fix on Tier 1/2 defects\n- Security boundaries, credential handling, and hard stops\n\n**Out of scope:**\n- BrowserStack Automate configuration (covered in [Guide 3](../../277807105/277676053))\n- Zephyr Scale result sync (covered in Guide 3)\n- The Playwright spec authoring workflow (covered in Guide 3)\n- PR merge — this always requires a human reviewer\n\n---\n\n2. Solution Overview\n--------------------\n\nThe agent operates as a **hybrid** — a CI-automated layer handles triage and administration for all failures without any human present, and an interactive Claude Code CLI skill handles investigation and fix for Tier 1/2 defects when a developer picks up the Jira ticket.\n\nThe split is designed around risk. Tier 3/4 files are squad-owned and feature-scoped — the blast radius of an incorrect automated fix is contained and reviewable via PR. Tier 1/2 files affect all storefronts and multiple squads — automated code generation on those files introduces unacceptable risk, so the agent stops at investigation and a developer owns the fix.\n\n```\nBrowserStack run completes (failures detected)\n          │\n          ▼\nCI: GitHub Actions — defect-triage.yml\n          │\n          ├── Bedrock (Haiku): classify 