del` endpoint.\n\n```\n// bedrock-invoke.js — simplified structure\nimport { BedrockRuntimeClient, InvokeModelCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-bedrock-runtime'\n\nconst client = new BedrockRuntimeClient({ region: process.env.AWS_BEDROCK_REGION })\n\nexport async function classify(failureReport) {\n  const command = new InvokeModelCommand({\n    modelId: 'anthropic.claude-haiku-20240307-v1:0',\n    body: JSON.stringify({\n      anthropic_version: 'bedrock-2023-05-31',\n      max_tokens: 512,\n      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: buildClassificationPrompt(failureReport) }]\n    }),\n    contentType: 'application/json',\n    accept: 'application/json'\n  })\n  const response = await client.send(command)\n  return JSON.parse(new TextDecoder().decode(response.body))\n}\n\nexport async function investigate(failureReport, fileContents, testCaseDetail) {\n  const command = new InvokeModelCommand({\n    modelId: 'anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5',\n    body: JSON.stringify({\n      anthropic_version: 'bedrock-2023-05-31',\n      max_tokens: 4096,\n      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: buildInvestigationPrompt(failureReport, fileContents, testCaseDetail) }]\n    }),\n    contentType: 'application/json',\n    accept: 'application/json'\n  })\n  const response = await client.send(command)\n  return JSON.parse(new TextDecoder().decode(response.body))\n}\n```\n\n### 5.3 Credential Handling\n\nAWS credentials are stored as GitHub Actions secrets and injected as environment variables into the CI job. They are never logged, never passed in LLM prompts, and never committed to source control.\n\n| Secret | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` | Bedrock IAM access key |\n| `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | Bedrock IAM secret |\n| `AWS_BEDROCK_REGION` | AWS region hosting the Bedrock endpoint (e.g. `us-east-1`) |\n\n### 5.4 IAM Boundary\n\nThe IAM role used by the CI job must be scoped to the minimum permissions required:\n\n* `bedrock:InvokeModel` on the specific model ARNs used (Haiku and Sonnet)\n* No `bedrock:ListFoundationModels`, no `bedrock:CreateModelCustomizationJob`\n* No access to any other AWS service from this role\n\n---\n\n6. GitHub Actions Workflow Design\n---------------------------------\n\n### 6.1 Trigger\n\nThe `defect-triage.yml` workflow triggers on completion of the BrowserStack execution job in `qa.yml`, conditional on failures being present:\n\n```\non:\n  workflow_run:\n    workflows: ['BrowserStack E2E']\n    types: [completed]\n\njobs:\n  triage:\n    if: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'failure' }}\n```\n\n### 6.2 Job Structure\n\n```\njobs:\n  parse-failures        # Extract failed test names, file paths, BrowserStack session URLs\n  classify-tier         # Run classify-tier.js — deterministic CODEOWNERS check\n  classify-failures     # Call Bedrock Haiku — code defect / env / test data per failure\n  create-jira-defects   # Jira REST API — one Jira ticket per failure (service account token)\n  investigate           # Call Bedrock Sonnet — RCA for code defects\n  create-branches       # GitHub API — fix branch per code defect\n  generate-fix          # Bedrock Sonnet — code fix for Tier 3/4 only\n  raise-prs             # GitHub API — PR per Tier 3/4 fix branch\n```\n\n`classify-tier` and `classify-failures` run in parallel after `parse-failures` completes. All downstream jobs depend on both completing.\n\n### 6.3 Jira REST API Integration\n\nJira defects are created using the Jira REST API v3 with a scoped service account token. The service account must have issue creation and linking permissions in the SFCC project only — no broader Jira access.\n\n```\n// jira-create-issue.js — simplified structure\nexport async function createDefect(payload) {\n  const response = await fetch(\n    'https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue',\n    {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: {\n        'Authorization': `Basic ${Buffer.from(\n          `${process.env.JIRA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL}:${process.env.JIRA_API_TOKEN}`\n        ).toString('base64')}`,\n        'Content-Type': 'application/json'\n      },\n      body: JSON.stringify(payload)\n    }\n  )\n  return response.json()\n}\n```\n\nAdditional GitHub Actions secrets required for Jira integration:\n\n| Secret | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `JIRA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL` | Email of the Jira service account used by the CI workflow |\n| `JIRA_API_TOKEN` | Jira API token scoped to issue create and link in the SFCC project |\n\n### 6.4 Jira Defect Content\n\nEach Jira defect created by the agent is pre-populated with:\n\n* **Summary:** `[STORY-KEY] [Test Case Name] — [Classification] — [Browser / Storefront]`\n* **Description:** failure message, stack trace, BrowserStack session recording link, root cause analysis (Sonnet output), affected file paths and their tier\n* **Labels:** `agent-raised`, `defect`, the classification, the storefront, the file tier\n* **Priority:** P1 if the failure blocks story sign-off; P2 otherwise\n* **Assignee:** Lead Developer (Tier 1), Squad Lead (Tier 2), unassigned (Tier 3/4 — PR raised instead)\n* **Linked to:** the originating Jira story and the Zephyr test case\n\n### 6.5 Fix Branch Naming\n\n| Tier | Branch name |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tier 1/2 (investigation only) | `fix/agent-investigation-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[short-description]` |\n| Tier 3/4 (auto-fix) | `fix/agent-autofix-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[short-description]` |\n\nThe `agent-investigation` vs `agent-autofix` prefix makes branch intent visible at a glance in the GitHub branch list and in PR titles.\n\n---\n\n7. Claude Code CLI Skill Design\n-------------------------------\n\n### 7.1 Purpose\n\nThe `/defect-triage [JIRA-KEY]` skill is invoked by a developer when they pick up a Tier 1/2 defect Jira ticket. It provides a structured, context-loaded starting point for the interactive fix session — removing the setup overhead of manually finding the failure detail, checking out the branch, and reading the investigation notes.\n\n### 7.2 What the Skill Does on Invocation\n\n```\nDeveloper runs: /defect-triage ZIM-DEF-089\n          │\n          ▼\nSkill reads JIRA-KEY from the command argument\n          │\n          ▼\nAtlassian MCP: retrieve the Jira defect ticket\n— failure detail, stack trace, BrowserStack session link\n— RCA from the CI investigation step\n— affected file paths and tier classification\n— linked Jira story and Zephyr test case\n          │\n          ▼\nGitHub MCP: check out the pre-created fix branch\nfix/agent-investigation-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[description]\n          │\n          ▼\nSkill presents a structured session brief to the developer:\n— Summary of the failure\n— The RCA the CI agent produced\n— The files involved and their tier\n— A proposed investigation approach\n— Explicit note: \"This is a Tier [1/2] file —\n  you own the fix. I will assist but will not write\n  to this file without your explicit instruction.\"\n          │\n          ▼\nDeveloper works interactively with Claude Code to\ninvestigate, implement a fix, and raise a PR\n```\n\n### 7.3 Skill Constraints\n\nThe skill observes the same tier rules as the CI agent:\n\n* Tier 1 files: Claude Code will analyse and propose a fix approach, but will not write to the file without the developer typing an explicit instruction to do so\n* Tier 2 files: Claude Code will implement a fix on developer instruction; the PR it raises will automatically carry the Tier 2 label and request a second reviewer\n* The skill will not suppress the tier warning or proceed past it silently\n\n---\n\n8. Security & Guardrails\n------------------------\n\n| Guardrail | Enforcement mechanism |\n| --- | --- |\n| No auto-merge | GitHub branch protection on `develop` and `release/**` — cannot be bypassed by any workflow or bot |\n| Tier 1 hard stop on code generation | `classify-tier.js` outputs tier before any Bedrock call; CI job conditional blocks fix generation step for Tier 1 |\n| Tier 2 auto-fix permitted but labelled | PR carries `tier-2-change` label; CODEOWNERS enforces second reviewer requirement |\n| No LLM tier classification | `classify-tier.js` is a deterministic CODEOWNERS lookup — not a Bedrock call |\n| Credentials never in LLM context | Bedrock and Jira credentials injected as CI environment variables; prompt construction never includes credential values |\n| Agent-raised PRs are identifiable | All agent PRs carry the `agent-raised` label; branch name prefix `fix/agent-` is enforced in the workflow |\n| Jira writes use a scoped service account | The CI workflow Jira service account has issue create and link permissions only — no admin, no bulk operations, no other projects |\n| CodeRabbit reviews agent PRs | `.coderabbit.yaml` applies the same review rules to agent-raised PRs as to human-raised PRs — assertive profile, tier warnings, test coverage checks |\n\n---\n\n9. Open Decisions\n-----------------\n\n| # | Decision | Status | Resolution | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| OD-01 | AWS Bedrock region | 🔴 Open | `us-east-1` (lowest latency to GitHub Actions runners) vs `ap-southeast-2` (closer to Zimmermann AU operations) | Integration Lead |\n| OD-02 | IAM role provisioning | 🔴 Open | Zimmermann IT provisions the IAM role and delivers credentials to Accenture as GitHub secrets, vs Accenture provisions under a temporary sandbox account | Zimmermann IT + Integration Lead |\n| OD-03 | Jira writes in CI context — MCP vs REST API | ✅ Resolved | The Atlassian MCP requires a persistent Claude Desktop session and cannot be called from a GitHub Actions runner. **Decision: CI workflow uses the Jira REST API v3 with a scoped service account token for all automated Jira writes. The Atlassian MCP is used only by the `/defect-triage` Claude Code CLI skill in interactive developer sessions.** Registry governance principle updated to reflect this boundary. | Integration Lead |\n| OD-04 | Sonnet model version on Bedrock | 🔴 Open | Confirm which Claude Sonnet version is available in the selected Bedrock region and whether it requires a provisioned throughput commitment at expected CI run volumes | Integration Lead |\n| OD-05 | P1 vs P2 severity assignment logic | 🔴 Open | Proposal: P1 if the failing test case is tagged Critical or High priority in Zephyr; P2 otherwise | Lead QA + Integration Lead |\n\n---\n\n*Last updated: June 2026 | Zimmermann Digital Modernisation — Accenture Song*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f4d0",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

